  faqHeading: "Accounting firm websites in the UAE: what partners ask first",
  faqs: [
    { question: "What should an accounting firm website in the UAE include?", answer: "One page per filing obligation you actually perform, pages for the client types whose handling genuinely differs, a credentials block with registration numbers a visitor can verify, fixed-fee pages with their scope bounded, short enquiry forms that capture entity type and deadline, and a document route that is not a partner's personal inbox." },
    { question: "How much does an accounting firm website cost in the UAE?", answer: `A practice site of up to 100 pages starts from ${P.site}, which suits most firms of two to ten people. Where every obligation, client type and emirate needs a page and the tree runs into the hundreds, that is an SEO site from ${P.seoSite}. A client document portal is a separate software project from ${P.software}. Everything is itemised in USD.` },
    { question: "Will you write our corporate tax and VAT service pages?", answer: "No. We are engineers in India with no accounting or tax qualification, so we will not write, edit, interpret or check a statement about tax. We build the structure, the templates, the forms and the review workflow, and your partners or a writer they appoint supply the words. If another supplier offers to write that content, ask who is qualified to review it." },
    { question: "How should we show that we are an FTA-registered tax agent?", answer: "Publish the registration number itself, name the register it appears on, and link to the lookup where one exists, so a visitor can confirm it without contacting you. Hold it as a data record with the holder and any expiry attached rather than as text typed into several templates, so a lapse is one field to change." },
    { question: "Should our fees be on the website?", answer: "Publish a starting fee for work that genuinely packages, with its boundary stated beside it: the transaction volume assumed, one entity and one bank account, and what moves a client to a quoted engagement. A bounded published fee attracts better enquiries than request a quote. Leave cleanup work for unseen records unpriced, and say why." },
    { question: "Why do all UAE tax service pages look the same, and how do we rank?", answer: "Because most of them restate the law, list the same benefits and end with the same free consultation, leaving search engines to pick between near-duplicates on authority alone. What breaks out is detail only a practising firm has: the document nobody expects, what a late registration really involves, the mistakes you fix most often, written as self-contained answers." },
    { question: "How do we stop the figures on our website going out of date?", answer: "Give every statement of a figure a visible as-at date, a named owner in the firm and a review interval, and make an overdue list the partners can see. Better still, link to the authority's own published source instead of keeping a private copy. We build that workflow; the sign-off on every number stays with your partners." },
    { question: "Is a free consultation the right call to action for an accounting firm?", answer: "It is the default and it is weak, because every competitor offers it and it attracts the least committed enquiries. Stronger options are a priced diagnostic with a written output, a published fee range with bounded scope, or a short routed form. If you keep a free call, scope it on the page: twenty minutes to establish whether there is an engagement." },
    { question: "Do we need a client portal for documents?", answer: `Eventually, not necessarily now. A portal gives per-client isolation, a full upload and download log, a requested-documents list with status, and retention that actually deletes, and it starts from ${P.software}. For a small practice with few clients, a disciplined shared drive and a written request checklist gets most of the value. Build it when chasing documents is costing real hours.` },
    { question: "Can the website collect identity documents for client onboarding?", answer: "It should not, before an engagement exists, because you would then hold sensitive personal data for someone who may never become a client. Your own compliance officer defines what is collected and how it is stored. The website's useful role is listing what a new client will be asked for and starting the process, not performing the checks." },
    { question: "Does an accounting firm website need an Arabic version?", answer: "It depends on the client base. Most UAE practices launch in English because that is how finance teams and documents work. Arabic earns its place when you serve Emirati-owned businesses and family groups, government-adjacent entities, or a trading community that enquires in Arabic. A frozen Arabic section that stopped being updated is worse than none." },
    { question: "Do you have an office or any accounting qualification in the UAE?", answer: "No to both. BtechWaleTech is three freelance engineers working remotely from India, with no UAE office, telephone number, trade licence, staff or tax agent registration, and no accounting qualification in any jurisdiction. We cannot attend your premises or advise on tax. India is 1.5 hours ahead of Dubai, so calls happen inside your working day." },
  ],
